## 2.3.4 Metal Insertion
Metal insertion is a convenient method to obtain various MM-MOFs that can be applied in various MOF materials (Figure 13). In homometallic anionic-framework MOFs the desired different metal ions can be inserted by cation exchange.[84-86] Also, appropriate functional groups (e.g. chelating sites, thioether functional group, etc.) in MOFs can load metal ions or lead to the inclusion of metal complexes by soaking the parent MOF in solutions of metal ions or complexes (Figure 14).[86-89]
This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
Loading of metal nanoparticles or organometallic compounds in MOF cavities is another type of metal insertion which according to the adopted definition of these materials also leads to the preparation of MM-MOFs. [90-93] After inclusion of metal ions into the MOF pores, metal nanoparticles inside of MOF cavities will be obtained by applying reducing reagent or photoreduction.[94, 95] Moreover, chemical or electrochemical reduction of MOFs using organometallic reducing agents or by applying negative potentials in an electrode leads to the preparation of mixed-valence MOFs which can subsequently uptake in the cavities new metal ions or organometallic complexes. [96, 97]
